// @@@ If the result is not null, then the previous value associated with the key is equal to the result.

We are in method put(K key, V value) returning V (previous value). The comment says: If the result (i.e., \result) is not null, then the previous value associated with the key is equal to the result.

We need to refer to "previous value associated with the key". That's the value that was stored before this put operation. We can get that using old map state: \old(this.get(key)) maybe? But after put, the map now contains value (new value). The previous value was the one before modification. Since we have observer method get(Object key) (public V get(Object key)), we can use \old(this.get(key)). However note that if key was null, the method goes to putForNullKey(value). The comment likely assumes general case; but we can still use \old(this.get(key)). For key null case, get(null) works as per map semantics. So assert: (\result != null) => \old(this.get(key)).equals(\result) ? Need equality semantics: V may be object; we can use ==? Usually they use .equals for objects. In examples they used .equals for query elements. For value equality, we can use \old(this.get(key)).equals(\result) if not null. But \old(this.get(key)) could be null as well; but premise is \result != null, then old value must be equal to result. So we can assert: (\result != null) => \old(this.get(key)).equals(\result). However need to ensure we don't call .equals on null; but premise ensures \result not null; old value could be null? If old value was null, then result would be null? Actually if previous value was null, then result is null, not non-null. So old value non-null is implied. So safe.

